#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9c5a01c-0ee5-5bae-8888-ff53dc6cd7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347776} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0b74d2-34af-5d66-903a-9cdd5c735154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347777} {:answer/id #uuid "91f18d9b-cc19-568d-8e13-b2de5e15c0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347778} {:answer/id #uuid "d12c34ba-e62c-5240-bf84-931a086135da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347779} {:answer/id #uuid "92191c64-1193-55fd-9101-e7181e9e152d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec0b74d2-34af-5d66-903a-9cdd5c735154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347777} :answer-field/id #uuid "92d7995d-8db5-576b-bf0c-8149732657d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5749657-28d3-56b8-bb44-5a5929a89f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104416" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2+1)-(1-2)+0 \\\\  & =-3+1+0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1-4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-5)=\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91bbefe7-ed13-535e-92df-53f8f98265ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347783} {:answer/id #uuid "81a52357-0ef1-5563-a3fa-115cb5ccac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347784} {:answer/id #uuid "d21864fb-dc96-560d-bbad-6c2e2eb12d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347785} {:answer/id #uuid "6170c848-6c8b-5181-92d2-797af55a069d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347786} {:answer/id #uuid "74e332f4-3f7a-515c-bf6b-1b0bb8cb7390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21864fb-dc96-560d-bbad-6c2e2eb12d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347785} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f8d4ac4-b27f-597f-a0de-37df5c34ace9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f852a7f-774b-52bd-b4f1-2d3498d047cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104439" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & k & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=k-4.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-4\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{22}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{k-4} \\\\ k-4 & =-4 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d5272a-0cab-5376-8cc2-9846d1a9732d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057207} {:answer/id #uuid "db6aa180-4113-5a47-8b2b-e7aff223bf88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057208} {:answer/id #uuid "fe205c54-add7-583e-8ac1-32bb54175b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057209} {:answer/id #uuid "33383130-ee92-5b06-a3a4-6e07a8f561ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057210} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6dbc16-b3a6-59d8-b730-c27a59bc6ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6aa180-4113-5a47-8b2b-e7aff223bf88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057208} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d1ff0b-1b6c-58d0-ae71-cd46a787c6fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce77ea41-d1eb-55f8-914d-e1a94a42a6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-18279" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bee45de-21b1-5b89-9fb2-3e4a0db089c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bee45de-21b1-5b89-9fb2-3e4a0db089c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347790} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9bdc86-f7f0-522f-b5f0-f9aaa77b72e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cba02839-f607-5bca-bb5a-b26b03338633" :question/math-academy-id "q-319420" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{31}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2+1)-0\\,(0-1)+2\\,(-0-2) \\\\  & =3-0-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}^{t}={c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "604e9b23-51c3-5262-b35b-86f8a3816257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310569} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbf0f49-2d96-5783-b73d-91a17a0d1679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "604e9b23-51c3-5262-b35b-86f8a3816257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310569} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b25f54-747f-5260-9908-969b50f76fbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4685104e-5c8e-5535-9bd4-12467257f628" :question/math-academy-id "q-319859" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\,(0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot k)+(-1)\\,(0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =(2+1)-2\\,(-k)+(-1)\\,(-k) \\\\  & =3+2k+k \\\\  & =3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-(4-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3k+3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3k+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3}{3k+3} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3k-3 & =-6 \\\\ -3k & =-6+3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
